/-!
# `Rf_*` commutes with derived completion: the commutation itself, and Equation 52.7.0.1

[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] reads *"`Rf_*` commutes with derived completion"*, and states the conclusion
as the display

`Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧`

which [Stacks, Tag 0A0I] records as Equation 52.7.0.1. Both of those are statements about the
two *functors* `Rf_* ⋙ (-)^∧` and `(-)^∧ ⋙ Rf_*`, and about the two *objects* `(Rf_*K)^∧` and
`Rf_*(K^∧)`; neither is on its face a statement that a particular morphism between them is
invertible.

`PushforwardCommutesCompletionLocalToGlobal.lean` proves that the canonical comparison map of
[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] is invertible at every object, at the tag's own data — a sheaf of ideals
`ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` of **finite type** and `ℐ' = f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X`. This file turns that pointwise
invertibility into the two statements the source actually displays:

* the natural transformation `Rf_* ⋙ (-)^∧ ⟶ (-)^∧ ⋙ Rf_*` is invertible, so the two functors
  are isomorphic — this is the commutation of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] read as the commutation of
  functors it is phrased as;
* the resulting isomorphism of functors, and its component `(Rf_*K)^∧ ≅ Rf_*(K^∧)`, which is
  Equation 52.7.0.1 at an object.

## Main results

* `isIso_natTrans_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_inverseImageIdealSheaf_of_isFiniteType`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0A0G]** — `Rf_*` commutes with derived completion, as the invertibility of the
  canonical natural transformation, at a sheaf of ideals of finite type.
* `derivedCompletionPushforwardIsoOfIsFiniteType`: that commutation as an isomorphism of
  functors `Rf_* ⋙ (-)^∧ ≅ (-)^∧ ⋙ Rf_*`.
* `derivedCompletionPushforwardObjIsoOfIsFiniteType`: **Equation 52.7.0.1 of
  [Stacks, Tag 0A0I]** at an object, `(Rf_*K)^∧ ≅ Rf_*(K^∧)`.

## Implementation notes

Packaging the pointwise statement as an isomorphism of functors is not a formality here: the
display identifies two *functors* of `K`, so the identification has to be natural in `K`, and
that is what `CategoryTheory.NatIso.isIso_of_isIso_app` supplies from the comparison map being a
natural transformation to begin with. The isomorphism is moreover the canonical one — it is the
comparison map of [Stacks, Tag 0A0G], characterised by its compatibility with the two
completion units in
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionUnit_comp_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison`
— and not merely some abstract isomorphism between the two sides, which is what makes it an
identification of the display's two sides rather than a statement that they happen to be
isomorphic.

*Hypotheses.* Exactly those of `PushforwardCommutesCompletionLocalToGlobal.lean`, which are the
source's: a morphism of ringed **sites**, a sheaf of ideals `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` of finite type, and `ℐ'` the
ideal sheaf `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` that Section 52.7 builds out of `ℐ` — whose own finite type, and
whose relation to `ℐ` of [Stacks, Tag 099J], are proved rather than assumed. There is **no**
hypothesis on the object, **no** derived-completeness hypothesis, **no** hypothesis that `Rf_*`
applied to the completion unit is invertible, and **no** assumption that `ℐ` is generated by
global sections or that its local sections are nilpotent. The instance binders are the standing
sheafification and total-complex hypotheses of this directory, discharged at a ringed space and
hence at a scheme.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 099F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099F)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099J](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0G](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0G)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0I)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

